        ["A chart audit finds that timed minutes do not match billed units in 30% of notes. What is the BEST next step?", ["Retrain staff and re-audit, and assess whether refunds are needed", "Adjust future notes to match the units already billed", "Stop auditing, because errors are common in all clinics", "Remove timed codes from the clinic's billing entirely"], "Audit findings call for education, follow-up auditing, and review of whether overpayments must be returned."],
        ["Which statement BEST describes a safe harbor under the Anti-Kickback Statute?", ["An arrangement that meets all conditions is protected", "Any arrangement is legal if both parties sign a contract", "Small kickbacks are allowed if under a dollar threshold", "Referrals are allowed if the patient agrees in writing"], "Safe harbors protect only arrangements that meet every specified condition, such as fair market value rent in writing."],
        ["A PT applies a hot pack and the patient develops a burn. Which risk management step is MOST important for preventing the next burn?", ["Review the thermal agent procedure and check staff competency", "Ban all thermal agents from the clinic permanently", "Have patients sign a waiver before any modality", "Tell staff to apply hot packs for shorter periods"], "Risk management fixes the process and checks competency. Waivers do not prevent burns or remove liability for negligence."],
        ["Which feature makes the Stark Law different from the Anti-Kickback Statute?", ["Stark is strict liability, so intent does not matter", "Stark applies only to commercial insurance payers", "Stark is criminal while Anti-Kickback is civil only", "Stark allows payment for referrals in small amounts"], "Stark is a civil, strict liability law. Anti-Kickback requires knowing and willful conduct and carries criminal penalties."],
        ["A front desk employee learns that the clinic routinely bills Medicare for visits that never happened. Which provision allows her to sue on the government's behalf?", ["The qui tam provision of the False Claims Act", "The safe harbor rule of the Anti-Kickback Statute", "The in-office ancillary exception of the Stark Law", "The minimum necessary standard under HIPAA"], "Qui tam lets whistleblowers file suit and share in recoveries under the False Claims Act."],
        ["Which aim statement is BEST written for a quality improvement project?", ["Reduce no-shows from 18% to 10% by June 30", "Improve the clinic's attendance as soon as possible", "Make patients happier with their care this year", "Train staff to work harder on scheduling issues"], "A strong aim is specific, measurable, and time-bound."],
        ["After a PDSA test, near misses fell from 6 to 1. Which action fits the Act step?", ["Adopt the change and update the policy", "Write a prediction for the next test", "Collect another month of baseline data", "Run the test again with no changes"], "Act decides whether to adopt, adapt, or abandon. A clear improvement supports adoption."],
        ["A marketing company offers to pay a clinic a fee for every patient it refers from a Medicare Advantage plan. What should the owner do FIRST?", ["Get a legal review before agreeing to any arrangement", "Accept, because Medicare Advantage is a private plan", "Accept, if the fee is less than the visit payment", "Decline only if the referrals are below 10 per month"], "Per-referral payments involving federal program patients raise Anti-Kickback concerns. Legal review comes before signing."]
